    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d449-a1">

                <pb facs="1020" n="978" xml:id="pg978"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">Annex</seg>
                </opener>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Paper Prepared in the Department of State</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content-and-sibling-dates"
                            calendar="gregorian" notAfter="1951-10-18T23:59:59-05:00"
                            notBefore="1951-09-27T00:00:00-04:00">undated</date>.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>It is recommended that in view of the very serious ramifications of this proposal
                    that Churchill should be warned not to
                    proceed hastily or publicly on it. It is felt that the best way to accomplish
                    this would be to instruct Gifford to
                    raise the matter with him or with the new British Foreign Secretary if the
                    Conservatives win the election immediately thereafter. A draft of a proposed
                    instruction to London along this line follows:</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“We have noted with some concern Churchill’s refs in speeches to possible mtg between the
                        President, Churchill and Stalin. After election results announced and
                        if Cons win, you shld seek early opportunity to discuss this matter with
                            Churchill or new FonMin pointing out that there shld be no
                        hasty public move re mtg with Stalin
                        before discussions with us. You cld say that there are many pro and cons to
                        desirability of such mtg which wld need to be carefully weighed and which we
                        wld wish discuss thoroughly with Brit prior possible public proposal.”</p>
                </quote>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
